#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "5b83575b-9674-5dc5-b3eb-a918f145be93" :question/math-academy-id "e-497" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2{x}}{x} \\textrm{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x-2I \\\\ 3I & ={ln}^{3}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "72d0b4b3-bfc6-5339-809f-ff7cd63e4d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-498" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-I \\\\ 2I & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "f415cae9-6b18-53c1-8c47-6e0a1f0de59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7313" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2} e^x\\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I = \\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin(2x)\\, \\textrm{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos(2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I\\mathbin.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))}{5}-\\frac{{e}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0))}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi /2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d508bf-d0e9-5547-8142-7e470d99c239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247925} {:answer/id #uuid "47d380dd-09a8-5053-8da1-0a1ae0b16a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248063} {:answer/id #uuid "55e95b28-54e7-5b51-a3bc-4ba2e89eb519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248203} {:answer/id #uuid "90961f5a-dd57-5f40-8e5f-e2727bb2f67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248709} {:answer/id #uuid "e0365e69-99c7-506d-a0be-882ad819a4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0365e69-99c7-506d-a0be-882ad819a4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249409} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc697f95-986c-5836-a641-1ed3e8f5e7b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ca1f5ad-5834-5a52-8770-140ed254c545" :question/math-academy-id "q-114950" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{e}^{-3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-3{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{e}^{-3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-3{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3J \\\\ I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3(-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a44699f-24c8-5a58-ba2d-76fbbc86ccec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{7}⁡x}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547517} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7a4366-3be3-5b98-9a3e-ba4a656574ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547583} {:answer/id #uuid "8e73f739-d875-5435-8483-802c12bd03ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547633} {:answer/id #uuid "f5af7b76-5c7d-52a9-8262-1e2bfb500412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{7}⁡x}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547815} {:answer/id #uuid "fc69b155-6de6-5e6d-a369-6a5bbeeb4817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7a4366-3be3-5b98-9a3e-ba4a656574ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547583} :answer-field/id #uuid "4338b54c-776e-5c97-9d8a-821ddad0e1e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d34958ec-5dd9-5a10-8bd6-7504a0d293c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114951" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{8}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{8{ln}^{7}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{8}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{8{ln}^{7}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8I \\\\ 9I & ={ln}^{9}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fa41e6-4e3a-5806-859d-c2aaa4b58edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247725} {:answer/id #uuid "3aad9910-8581-517c-bee6-27e599bcdafd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247943} {:answer/id #uuid "81ff4d92-1a10-5646-988f-1c901a51022f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248576} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6e5a27-6ad8-5b1a-b327-7847f10b1222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248844} {:answer/id #uuid "cefcf51a-b70a-5c3a-a3be-b1a5d98e9adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81ff4d92-1a10-5646-988f-1c901a51022f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248576} :answer-field/id #uuid "6117be46-83ef-580f-84ce-56c76fa2d084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f09819e-da12-5415-9b7c-4388f9ed375d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114952" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2I \\\\ 3I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{12}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec2f68f-3a5c-5320-80df-4677e1be6e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }-1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547347} {:answer/id #uuid "6081e439-1860-5222-91b9-aee228c648b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }+2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547525} {:answer/id #uuid "842dae31-aac6-5e10-b497-9abe5c499c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi /2}-1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547599} {:answer/id #uuid "8bff236b-251c-5407-acdf-108c7f7dd322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547623} {:answer/id #uuid "c07201a8-c8e4-5b0a-831a-ee4bce670816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bff236b-251c-5407-acdf-108c7f7dd322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547623} :answer-field/id #uuid "2392f4ba-bd38-5a07-8e70-90353132c90f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "509669b5-ef46-5eb1-8d54-7bbaca5f8fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115002" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})))-(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-0}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0340ba7d-60fe-5ce5-94fe-c3642864b856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{-\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547323} {:answer/id #uuid "200d0628-556e-5baa-ac44-a3d113c85b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547379} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3d7672-ea17-5f1b-946d-cbc34bf9be65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-2\\pi }-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547560} {:answer/id #uuid "99e7c620-6fc1-5713-836c-454ba4907213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547652} {:answer/id #uuid "b036bbd1-1c23-5d6d-9a9d-9e351a182b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{-2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e7c620-6fc1-5713-836c-454ba4907213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547652} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abcc4ca-4f5a-5ee4-b3d4-9f4549d9ca5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b786a85-3015-50d7-b6da-f38d91ed719a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115003" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2(-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-2\\pi /2}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})))-(\\frac{{e}^{-2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e5024b-1c1c-54cb-83fb-120b748a9ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }+2{e}^{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547322} {:answer/id #uuid "423c048e-ea22-5f3a-ad4f-122dccb7ad0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{-2\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547462} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9bb87c-c499-5aa9-adb7-e327fbd1e96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }-{e}^{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547484} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2c4c31-6263-5a76-9ab5-f8cd0d8c8d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547660} {:answer/id #uuid "d48a3ee5-6a7b-5611-a42f-6f64ee2d3a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2\\pi }-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a9bb87c-c499-5aa9-adb7-e327fbd1e96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }-{e}^{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547484} :answer-field/id #uuid "feba3f58-6495-56d8-aeb6-29990aa6bdd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce3e4235-61fa-5c48-8fde-1e9523a76255" :question/math-academy-id "q-115005" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+J \\\\ I & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I \\\\ 2I & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ))-(\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }-{e}^{\\pi }}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e5f1481-f060-5e2c-a1ba-9d650eb0364e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547410} {:answer/id #uuid "3de57636-1ffa-509b-8522-11db772f0631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547454} {:answer/id #uuid "6cede503-5875-5d7e-acae-646cdb24f91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547558} {:answer/id #uuid "8abaac36-eba4-5279-995b-1ce237e32fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547619} {:answer/id #uuid "c80ff131-1ac8-5344-98c3-286bacff57ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5f1481-f060-5e2c-a1ba-9d650eb0364e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547410} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6ff2439-5d4f-5486-b23d-8199292dfe50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "204edd9f-19f2-513e-ac33-9db65e721229" :question/math-academy-id "q-12924" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15738e1f-34bd-59f4-a55c-8499650f1ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272580} {:answer/id #uuid "38720872-3115-5e52-98be-18fa60ea6f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272880} {:answer/id #uuid "523a702e-9370-50b1-be12-45b0487956fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273085} {:answer/id #uuid "52b2827c-d7e6-5899-9970-1a8728feafba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273090} {:answer/id #uuid "6560bc1c-69a9-5f68-95b3-05e5e847af5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38720872-3115-5e52-98be-18fa60ea6f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272880} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e1a454-f080-5b1e-95e7-f11717670a46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcd130a5-30d7-5a4d-8994-16c748037d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-15409" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386e98c1-73bb-5454-b219-d1a0b2cbcd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312432} {:answer/id #uuid "8d06227a-dba4-573b-8ee8-7fe7ab605c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313101} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8ccfeb-d107-5fd5-a633-7ce78226c89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313120} {:answer/id #uuid "eba8d785-f5ae-5b9c-bb6e-c13c6ab5bfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313855} {:answer/id #uuid "f49e2812-02a9-51d6-8932-6f7fc008518b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "386e98c1-73bb-5454-b219-d1a0b2cbcd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312432} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e94abc-25eb-5e8f-9907-5fdc535a7393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84d99744-2a44-5b9d-88cd-43a5f0c291b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-3397" :question/problem "$\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{9}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a1bf69d-6891-560f-b34a-b2a74af9646e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547397} {:answer/id #uuid "64649790-f668-59d7-9c45-e3aba8bd1d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547539} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6b83e9-8074-57f6-8288-2a2fc643233f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547620} {:answer/id #uuid "a3428012-52f2-5611-af94-dbc2d0e4d7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547674} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbcaaaa-e8c2-5f47-afab-364c965f5b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdbcaaaa-e8c2-5f47-afab-364c965f5b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547839} :answer-field/id #uuid "7665ab87-9f58-54f0-86a0-f924247bce54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "706f56b6-76a3-5149-b5e7-35082287eff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-3402" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-J \\\\ I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I \\\\ 2I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff5d37fa-afb4-58b9-ac62-05838ab2df59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5d37fa-afb4-58b9-ac62-05838ab2df59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547842} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a039a4-2ad9-58c4-9de9-69c93ba57a90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d5e523f-cb45-5cb6-96e4-37ea7521523c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348848" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{4}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{4}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3})(\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4I \\\\ 5I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{15}({ln}^{5}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}({ln}^{5}⁡({e}^{5})-{ln}^{5}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}((5\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5}-{0}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}({5}^{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c033b7b5-f0bb-5912-ac93-d7393c96dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c033b7b5-f0bb-5912-ac93-d7393c96dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547729} :answer-field/id #uuid "1146e643-4cd0-54ef-a3e2-eff97703cea4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d4ef9d9-890a-5da6-8625-b613890df574" :question/math-academy-id "q-348879" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{4}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{6x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{4}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6})(\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4I \\\\ 5I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{30}({ln}^{5}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}({ln}^{5}⁡({e}^{3})-{ln}^{5}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}((3\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5}-{0}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}({3}^{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a13c3f8-7bec-5eb3-a18e-62df90a347e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321729} {:answer/id #uuid "cde87829-8161-59e8-a5a9-817dbc346072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-\\pi}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cde87829-8161-59e8-a5a9-817dbc346072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-\\pi}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547757} :answer-field/id #uuid "1926a9f5-5942-57bc-b6e6-d574c97fb17c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5475923-10f0-5e28-b504-857701a55831" :question/math-academy-id "q-362669" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =({e}^{-x})(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{10}(0-(-1)))-(\\frac{1}{10}(0-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{-\\pi }+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8493e63b-e686-55a1-be24-8c7387ac529a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067577} {:answer/id #uuid "b510294a-158b-59cf-9325-faaa6db5c999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067579} {:answer/id #uuid "b70392a5-b997-5c61-a383-809b3402a781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067580} {:answer/id #uuid "547cd2d2-426a-5ed4-ba68-b0d437d86899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067581} {:answer/id #uuid "1c227681-1e75-580c-a1f0-afa6524eba89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b510294a-158b-59cf-9325-faaa6db5c999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067579} :answer-field/id #uuid "2656a382-672f-58cb-ac31-49e3432a71cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d20f7fe1-3115-57de-8eab-3a56c473cbe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49976" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡z\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{1}{z}\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I \\\\ 4I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡z}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡z){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡(e)-{ln}^{4}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23815347-d93f-58ba-b1c7-e3ed64d48844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348752} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7073ff-ed85-58f2-b155-46bd8775ace3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349153} {:answer/id #uuid "8588b43f-4e08-5267-af90-836fa5f9e2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-\\pi /3}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349666} {:answer/id #uuid "92fc068b-a45e-5637-a0e8-f2f88ee3ad76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-\\pi /3}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349758} {:answer/id #uuid "a76c1c18-aaf5-5760-be34-52e784dcaf6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}({e}^{-\\pi /3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23815347-d93f-58ba-b1c7-e3ed64d48844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348752} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6af00b3-29da-55ac-a3ba-02da31bf42c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "742e2e60-8650-5364-af0a-ff31062d8fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-83014" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}))}{10}+\\frac{{e}^{-0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi /3}}{10}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
